WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.940
Breaking Bad's grand epilogue, El Camino,
is full of references to the beloved show

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.660
that preceded it, some more obvious than others.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.780
If you were too busy freaking out that you
finally got more Breaking Bad, here are some

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.680
small details in El Camino you may have missed.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.500
Beginning in the second season of Breaking
Bad, Walt and Jesse were frequently aided

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.480
and abetted in their illegal misdeeds by crooked
lawyer Saul Goodman, who operated out of an

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.620
unassuming office located in an Albuquerque
strip mall.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.410
At the end of the series, Saul abandoned his
legal practice and ran for his life, starting

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:35.720
over with a new identity in Nebraska.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.700
If you look closely, there is a brief callback
to Saul early on in El Camino, during the

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.660
night Jesse spends crashing at Skinny Pete's
house.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.900
In a series of brief time-lapse shots showing
the sun rising over Albuquerque, we're treated

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.520
to three shots of different locations in the
city.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:55.780
The second shows a nondescript strip mall,
but if it looks familiar, there's a reason

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.560
for that.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.640
One of the businesses shown is Duke City Sports
Bar, which resides in the same location that

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.460
used to house Saul Goodman &amp; Associates.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.360
Gus Fring is long dead by the time El Camino
rolls around, successfully killed off by Walt

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.640
in Season 4.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.920
While several other characters who perished
over the course of Breaking Bad still managed

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.320
to make an appearance in El Camino, Gus is
not among them.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.100
However, there is a blink-and-you'll-miss-it
nod to the show's greatest antagonist in the

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.620
same early morning sequence when we glimpse
Duke City Sports Bar.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:32.520
Right after the camera reveals the new business
occupying Saul's former office space, it shows

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.080
a fast food restaurant named "Twisters."

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.380
Twisters is in fact a real-life restaurant
in Albuquerque, which provided both the interior

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.400
and exterior shots for Los Pollos Hermanos
in Breaking Bad.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.920
In El Camino, it would seem that the fictional
restaurant has since morphed into its real-life

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:49.920
counterpart.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.580
"We take pride in everything that we do!"

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.159
One of the pivotal moments in Breaking Bad
occurred in the first half of season five,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:01.060
on the heels of Walt and Jesse's successful
train heist, which they carried out with Todd's

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.700
help.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.320
While they were still riding high from the
robbery, things took a sharp turn when they

00:02:05.329 --> 00:02:09.490
crossed paths with a young boy who'd been
out riding his bike in the desert and just

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.580
happened to come across the criminal trio.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.420
Although the child hadn't seen them commit
a crime, Todd still shot and killed him over

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.060
Jesse's frantic protests, later arguing that
no one was supposed to know that they'd been

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.230
in the desert at all.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.480
How does this connect to El Camino?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.070
It's subtle, but before the boy was killed,
he came across a tarantula, which he scooped

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.940
up in a glass jar, presumably to keep as a
pet.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:37.600
In El Camino, Jesse sees that Todd has a pet
tarantula, perhaps the very same tarantula,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.540
which he keeps in a desert-like terrarium
in his room.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.600
When he returns after Todd's death, a saddened
Jesse feeds the tarantula, no doubt thinking

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.540
of the child he was unable to save.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.700
Throughout the five seasons of Breaking Bad,
characters repeatedly hid things in places

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.120
where they hoped no one else would ever find
them.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:00.100
Stashing stuff in creative places only to
later have to move them or risk someone else

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:04.750
finding them was a recurring motif of the
show, so it was only fitting that El Camino

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:09.220
includes its own riff on the familiar theme
when Jesse rips apart Todd's apartment in

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.400
search of his drug money after Todd moves
it out of his World Book encyclopedias.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:17.800
One of the first places Jesse looks is under
Todd's kitchen sink.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.560
The location should be a familiar hiding place
for eagle-eyed Breaking Bad fans, since that's

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.860
where Jesse hid his stash of meth in season
two's frantic "Mandala".

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.280
Of course, the meticulous Todd had been a
little more inventive than Jesse and had actually

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.930
hidden his cash in the door of his refrigerator,
but Jesse's beeline for the sink was most

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.540
certainly a callback to the earlier days of
the show.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:44.360
Before Todd was a drug dealer and casual murderer,
he was an exterminator working for Vamonos

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.180
Pest.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.239
While Vamonos Pest was a legitimate pest control
company, its crew would often supplement their

00:03:50.239 --> 00:03:55.090
income by stealing from the houses they were
fumigating, or granting access to other criminals

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.250
for a price.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:04:01.019
Vamonos was eventually purchased by Walt,
Jesse, and Mike as a front for their meth

00:04:01.019 --> 00:04:06.230
cooking operation, and as a result, Todd became
increasingly entrenched in their business

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:08.080
before turning antagonist.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.220
El Camino includes a subtle nod to Todd's
pre-Breaking Bad profession in the scene where

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.000
the two Kandy Welding Company employees search
his apartment for his money, pretending to

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.420
be police officers.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:24.080
When Casey walks into Todd's bedroom, which
Jesse had utterly destroyed in his frantic

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.830
search for Todd's cash, Todd's Vamonos Pest
uniform can be seen in the foreground of the

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:31.300
shot, although the logo is only half visible.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:37.240
It's a stark reminder of how disastrous Heisenberg's
partnership with Vamonos Pest ultimately proved

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:43.020
to be, with Todd, Walt, and Mike all winding
up dead, and Jesse fleeing to Alaska.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:49.320
When Jesse realizes he's $1,800 short of what
he needs to start a new life, he decides that

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.900
instead of asking his friends or robbing his
parents, he'll instead go to the men of Kandy

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:58.640
Welding Company, who are as deep into illegal
activity as Jesse ever was.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.840
Earlier, Jesse convinced Neil Kandy to split
Todd's money with him three ways, knowing

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.610
that they both had a vested interest in not
doing anything that would draw the attention

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:08.960
of the police.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:15.040
Although Jesse only asks for $1,800; a relatively
small amount, considering Neil's take was

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.890
likely somewhere in the vicinity of a quarter
of a million dollars; a coked-up Neil suggests

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:24.020
that the two of them decide who gets the money
in a winner-take-all old-fashioned shootout.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.120
"Like the Wild West?"

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.260
"Yeah.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:29.780
Like the Wild West."

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.860
Breaking Bad has often been referred to as
a modern-day Western, and this scene could

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:39.160
easily be a nod to the genre that inspired
much of the aesthetic of the series.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:44.180
However, the "Wild West" reference may be
doing double duty, possibly also winking at

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:49.520
Aaron Paul's upcoming role on HBO's Westworld,
another show that offers a post-modern take

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.520
on classic Western tropes.

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.440
Although little is known about Paul's role
on the sci-fi series as of the making of this

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:01.069
video, his mention of the "Wild West" in El
Camino may not only be tipping his hat toward

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:04.760
the show he's leaving behind, but also the
one yet to come.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.860
"Dude, you're on fire."

